        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "The issue is clear. When the UI calls `POST /api/plugins/install` with `{ packageName: \"@paperclipai/plugin-file-browser-example\", isLocalPath: true }`, the sidecar receives this and tries to resolve it as a local filesystem path. The sidecar's install handler sees `isLocalPath: true` and looks for a local directory at that path.\n\nBut in our CF implementation, we set `localPath = packageName` in the examples list and the UI sends `isLocalPath: true` with `packageName = example.localPath = \"@paperclipai/plugin-file-browser-example\"`.\n\n\nThe sidecar then treats this as a filesystem path and attempts to locate it at an absolute path like `/Users/migmac/Documents/GitHub/_ABM/paperclip/server/@paperclipai/plugin-file-browser-example`. The solution is to have the CF install handler recognize npm package names (those starting with `@` or not starting with `/`) and remove the `isLocalPath: true` flag before sending the request to the sidecar.\n\nActually, a better approach would be to intercept the request body in the CF handler, detect when `isLocalPath: true` is set but the package name is actually a valid npm package, and rewrite it to `isLocalPath: false` before proxying to the sidecar.\n\nLooking at how the CF plugins install endpoint works, it just passes the raw request through to the sidecar without any transformation. The real issue is that CF has no local filesystem, so I should just always treat plugin installs as npm installs and ignore the `isLocalPath` flag entirely. Let me implement that fix now.",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
